WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
Thirty of one hundred minutes belong to the task being accelerated.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
Doubling its speed reduces that part to fifteen; the other seventy remain unchanged.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:22.250
Total time becomes eighty-five minutes: improvement depends on the fraction actually affected.

4
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:29.917
Only twenty of one hundred cost units belong to the work receiving the improvement.

5
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:36.767
Reducing that cost by twenty percent saves four units.

6
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:46.317
Aggregate cost becomes ninety-six, not eighty; this is accounting, not a GDP forecast.

7
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:53.667
Three periods incur reorganization costs of eight, six and two units.

8
00:00:53.667 --> 00:01:01.967
Illustrative benefits are zero, four and ten: net balances are minus eight, minus two and plus eight.

9
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:10.383
Cumulative balance is still minus two; a positive final period proves neither payback nor future success.

10
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:17.733
Of ten delivered reports, six pass the quality criterion.

11
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:24.583
Another configuration delivers twenty, but only ten pass.

12
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:32.250
Volume doubles while valid results rise from six to ten. Preserve both denominators.

13
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:39.600
A workday uses six hours for production and two for review.

14
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.767
Production falls to four hours, review stays at two and two hours are freed.

15
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:54.817
That time can go to more work or another use; it is not automatically output growth.

16
00:01:55.317 --> 00:02:02.167
Two groups start at one hundred; the tool-adopting group reaches one hundred twenty.

17
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:09.017
The comparison group reaches one hundred ten without adopting.

18
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:16.367
The difference in changes is ten; attribution requires assumptions such as parallel trends.

